: \nL24:   * cite7†Login L25:   * cite4†Help Pages†info.arxiv.org L26:   * cite8†About†info.arxiv.org L27: \nL28: # Computer Science > Human-Computer Interaction\nL29: \nL30: arXiv:2509.05718v1 (cs)\nL31: \nL32: [Submitted on 6 Sep 2025 (this version), latest version 7 Nov 2025 (cite9†v2 )]\nL33: # Title:Do Vision-Language Models See Visualizations Like Humans? Alignment in Chart Categorization\nL34: \nL35: Authors:cite10†Péter Ferenc Gyarmati , cite11†Manfred Klaffenböck , cite12†Laura Koesten , cite13†Torsten Möller L36: \nL37: View a PDF of the paper titled Do Vision-Language Models See Visualizations Like Humans? Alignment in Chart Categorization, by P\\'eter Ferenc Gyarmati and 3 other authors\nL38: \nL39: cite14†View PDF cite15†HTML (experimental) L40: > Abstract:Vision-language models (VLMs) hold promise for enhancing visualization tools, but effective human-AI collaboration hinges on a shared perceptual understanding of visual content. Prior studies assessed VLM visualization literacy through interpretive tasks, revealing an over-reliance on textual cues rather than genuine visual analysis. Our study investigates a more foundational skill underpinning such literacy: the ability of VLMs to recognize a chart's core visual properties as humans do.\nL41: We task 13 diverse VLMs with classifying scientific visualizations based solely on visual stimuli, according to three criteria: purpose (e.g., schematic, GUI, visualization), encoding (e.g., bar, point, node-link), and dimensionality (e.g., 2D, 3D). Using expert labels from the human-centric VisType typology as ground truth, we find that VLMs often identify purpose and dimensionality accurately but struggle with specific encoding types.\nL42: Our preliminary results show that larger models do not always equate to superior performance and highlight the need for careful integration of VLMs in visualization tasks, with human supervision to ensure reliable outcomes.\nL43: Comments: 2 pages, 2 figures. Accepted submission to the poster track of IEEE VIS 2025\nL44: Subjects: Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)\nL45: Cite as: cite16†arXiv:2509.05718 [cs.HC]\nL46:   (or  cite17†arXiv:2509.05718v1 [cs.HC] for this version)\nL47:   cite18†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.05718†doi.org L48: \nL49: Focus to learn more\nL50: \nL51:   arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL52: ## Submission history\nL53: \nL54: From: Péter Ferenc Gyarmati [cite19†view email ]\nL55: [v1] Sat, 6 Sep 2025 13:54:00 UTC (802 KB)\nL56: cite9†[v2] Fri, 7 Nov 2025 12:45:00 UTC (807 KB)\nL57: \nL58: Full-text links:\nL59: ## Access Paper:\nL60: \nL61:  View a PDF of the paper titled Do Vision-Language Models See Visualizations Like Humans? Alignment in Chart Categorization, by P\\'eter Ferenc Gyarmati and 3 other authors\nL62: \nL63:   * cite14†View PDF L64:   * cite15†HTML (experimental) L65:   * cite20†TeX Source  L66: \nL67: cite21† view license †creativecommons.org L68: \nL69: Current browse context:\nL70: \nL71: cs.HC\nL72: \nL73: cite22†< prev |   cite23†next > L74: \nL75: cite24†new |  cite25†recent | cite26†2025-09 L76: \nL77: Change to browse by:\nL78: \nL79: cite27†cs L80: ### References & Citations\nL81: \nL82:   * cite28†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L83:   * cite29†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L84:   * cite30†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L85: \nL86: export BibTeX citation Loading...\nL87: \nL88: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL89: \nL90: ×\nL91: \nL92: loading...\nL93: \nL94: Data provided by:\nL95: \nL96: ### Bookmark\nL97: \nL98: cite31† †www.bibsonomy.org cite32† †reddit.com L99: \nL100: Bibliographic Tools\nL101: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL102: \nL103: Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL104: \nL105: Bibliographic Explorer (cite33†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL106: \nL107: Connected Papers Toggle\nL108: \nL109: Connected Papers (cite34†What is Connected Papers?†www.connecte