to develop and share new arXiv features directly on our website.\nL185: \nL186: Both individuals and organizations that work with arXivLabs have embraced and accepted our values of openness, community, excellence, and user data privacy. arXiv is committed to these values and only works with partners that adhere to them.\nL187: Have an idea for a project that will add value for arXiv's community? cite49†Learn more about arXivLabs†info.arxiv.org .\nL188: \nL189: cite50†Which authors of this paper are endorsers? | Disable MathJax (cite51†What is MathJax?†info.arxiv.org )\nL190: \nL191:   * cite8†About†info.arxiv.org L192:   * cite4†Help†info.arxiv.org L193: \nL194:   * contact arXiv Click here to contact arXiv cite52† Contact†info.arxiv.org L195:   * subscribe to arXiv mailings Click here to subscribe cite53† Subscribe†info.arxiv.org L196:   * cite54†Copyright†info.arxiv.org L197:   * cite55†Privacy Policy†info.arxiv.org L198: \nL199:   * cite56†Web Accessibility Assistance†info.arxiv.org L200:   * cite57†arXiv Operational Status †status.arxiv.org --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2509.05545v1] Reinforcement Learning with Anticipation: A Hierarchical Approach for Long-Horizon Tasks (https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.05545v1)\nciteturn3210view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.05545v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 163\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Machine Learning\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 6 Sep 2025]\nL9: # Title:Reinforcement Learning with Anticipation: A Hierarchical Approach for Long-Horizon Tasks\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Yang Yu L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Reinforcement Learning with Anticipation: A Hierarchical Approach for Long-Horizon Tasks, by Yang Yu\nL14: \nL15: cite6†View PDF cite7†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Solving long-horizon goal-conditioned tasks remains a significant challenge in reinforcement learning (RL). Hierarchical reinforcement learning (HRL) addresses this by decomposing tasks into more manageable sub-tasks, but the automatic discovery of the hierarchy and the joint training of multi-level policies often suffer from instability and can lack theoretical guarantees.\nL17: In this paper, we introduce Reinforcement Learning with Anticipation (RLA), a principled and potentially scalable framework designed to address these limitations. The RLA agent learns two synergistic models: a low-level, goal-conditioned policy that learns to reach specified subgoals, and a high-level anticipation model that functions as a planner, proposing intermediate subgoals on the optimal path to a final goal.\nL18: The key feature of RLA is the training of the anticipation model, which is guided by a principle of value geometric consistency, regularized to prevent degenerate solutions. We present proofs that RLA approaches the globally optimal policy under various conditions, establishing a principled and convergent method for hierarchical planning and execution in long-horizon goal-conditioned tasks.\nL19: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite8†arXiv:2509.05545 [cs.LG]\nL21:    | (or cite9†arXiv:2509.05545v1 [cs.LG] for this version)\nL22:    | cite10†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.05545†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Yang Yu [cite11†view email ]\nL26: [v1] Sat, 6 Sep 2025 00:10:15 UTC (23 KB)\nL27: \nL28: Full-text links:\nL29: \nL30: ## Access Paper:\nL31: \nL32: View a PDF of the paper titled Reinforcement Learning with Anticipation: A Hierarchical Approach for Long-Horizon Tasks, by Yang Yu\nL33: \nL34: